1
00:00:00,050 --> 00:00:01,872
Wednesday morning.

2
00:00:01,922 --> 00:00:05,976
The money from the card network lands, and the bank is short.

3
00:00:06,026 --> 00:00:09,672
Not by much, but nobody can say which payment caused it.

4
00:00:09,722 --> 00:00:16,176
On Tuesday night, one of Malika's payments timed out on our side, but the processing centre booked it.

5
00:00:16,226 --> 00:00:20,280
Another we marked paid, but the centre reversed it overnight.

6
00:00:20,330 --> 00:00:22,488
Both look fine in our own records.

7
00:00:22,538 --> 00:00:33,168
In Lesson 1, ecom checked each payment against the processing centre's own record, and in Lesson 34, it counted its own side of each day.

8
00:00:33,218 --> 00:00:37,608
This lesson puts the two sides next to each other, line by line.

9
00:00:37,658 --> 00:00:41,832
Every day, for each card network, the bank compares.

10
00:00:41,882 --> 00:00:48,312
This is reconciliation: proving that two sets of books agree, line by line.

11
00:00:48,362 --> 00:00:56,328
Our side is every money movement ecom recorded: each charge, each reversal, each refund.

12
00:00:56,378 --> 00:01:00,480
Their side is the processing centre's own report for that day.

13
00:01:00,530 --> 00:01:06,576
ecom fetches that report through the card zone, and it arrives with masked card numbers only.

14
00:01:06,626 --> 00:01:11,712
ecom does the matching; the card zone only hands over the report.

15
00:01:11,762 --> 00:01:16,680
Lines are paired by kind, by payment, and by the network's reference number.

16
00:01:16,730 --> 00:01:24,600
Each pair must then agree on four things: whether money moved, the amount, the terminal, and the day.

17
00:01:24,650 --> 00:01:26,664
The day's totals are checked too.

18
00:01:26,714 --> 00:01:28,728
Any difference is a break.

19
00:01:28,778 --> 00:01:35,880
A break is one line where the two books disagree, or a line that one side has and the other does not.

20
00:01:35,930 --> 00:01:42,920
A payment still unknown when the day closes is a break as well.

21
00:01:42,970 --> 00:01:46,208
So what should happen to a difference once it is found?

22
00:01:46,258 --> 00:01:52,256
Every break becomes exactly one exception: a case with an owner and a deadline.

23
00:01:52,306 --> 00:01:55,016
I think this is the part that matters most.

24
00:01:55,066 --> 00:01:59,960
A difference that nobody owns is a difference that nobody fixes.

25
00:02:00,010 --> 00:02:01,832
Then a person works it.

26
00:02:01,882 --> 00:02:08,000
If they find proof that the two sides really agree, they resolve it with that evidence.

27
00:02:08,050 --> 00:02:13,520
If the difference is real and cannot be recovered, they ask to write it off.

28
00:02:13,570 --> 00:02:18,464
A write-off means the bank accepts the difference and closes the case.

29
00:02:18,514 --> 00:02:23,768
Because a write-off is money the bank gives up, someone else must decide it.

30
00:02:23,818 --> 00:02:27,248
Every change to an exception is written down.

31
00:02:27,298 --> 00:02:31,304
Dilnoza opens Reconciliation in the bank's portal.

32
00:02:31,354 --> 00:02:37,880
She chooses a card network and Tuesday as the business day, and presses Reconcile.

33
00:02:37,930 --> 00:02:43,832
A run like this happens every night on its own; this button runs a day again.

34
00:02:43,882 --> 00:02:45,704
The run finishes.

35
00:02:45,754 --> 00:02:47,576
Totals shows Differ.

36
00:02:47,626 --> 00:02:55,376
The items list shows every line with its match state: matched, mismatched, or missing on one side.

37
00:02:55,426 --> 00:02:59,408
A mismatched line gives its reason: amount differs.

38
00:02:59,458 --> 00:03:03,296
Our amount and the network's amount sit side by side.

39
00:03:03,346 --> 00:03:05,912
Dilnoza opens Exceptions.

40
00:03:05,962 --> 00:03:10,976
Every unmatched item is here, with its owner and when it is due.

41
00:03:11,026 --> 00:03:12,848
One is marked Overdue.

42
00:03:12,898 --> 00:03:17,624
She presses Take, and the page says: you own this exception now.

43
00:03:17,674 --> 00:03:20,048
She adds a note about what she checked.

44
00:03:20,098 --> 00:03:25,712
The difference is real, so she writes the reason and chooses Ask to write off.

45
00:03:25,762 --> 00:03:28,784
The page says a second admin decides.

46
00:03:28,834 --> 00:03:33,632
When Dilnoza looks at the exception again, there is no approve button for her.

47
00:03:33,682 --> 00:03:40,736
Instead, a line says she asked to write this off, and another admin approves or rejects.

48
00:03:40,786 --> 00:03:46,544
A second admin logs in, opens the same exception, and reads the reason.

49
00:03:46,594 --> 00:03:48,416
They approve the write-off.

50
00:03:48,466 --> 00:03:50,600
The state becomes Written off.

51
00:03:50,650 --> 00:03:58,472
The audit trail at the bottom shows every step: who opened it, who took it, who asked, and who approved.

52
00:03:58,522 --> 00:04:05,696
Without reconciliation, two books can disagree for weeks, and the bank learns about it from a buyer's complaint.

53
00:04:05,746 --> 00:04:16,496
With it, Tuesday's difference is found on Wednesday, it has one owner and a deadline, and it cannot disappear because one person decided to ignore it.

54
00:04:16,546 --> 00:04:22,736
That protects the bank's money, and it protects Malika from a payment lost between two systems.

55
00:04:22,786 --> 00:04:28,736
To recap: every difference gets one owner, and giving money up needs a second admin.

56
00:04:28,786 --> 00:04:33,304
So who finally decides a write-off?

57
00:04:33,354 --> 00:04:36,400
A second admin, never the one who asked.

58
00:04:36,450 --> 00:04:39,928
Next: limits on what one website may take.
